How it works
Four stages, each a separate, swappable module:
1. Attention map — compute vorticity magnitude (curl of a 2D/3D vector field) or gradient magnitude (scalar fields), normalize to [0, 1]. High attention = shock fronts, vortex cores, chaotic boundaries. Low attention = smooth, laminar background.
2. Adaptive bit-packing — route each voxel to a precision tier by its attention score (4-bit → 8-bit → 16-bit → lossless float32 passthrough), quantize per tier, pack tightly.
3. Constraint alignment — solve in closed form for the multiplicative correction that makes the reconstructed field's total energy (or any sum-of-squares-type invariant) match the original exactly; apply it only to the lossy background tiers, leaving high-precision regions untouched.
4. Entropy coding — serialize the multi-precision tiered stream and run it through zstandard, benchmarked against zstd/gzip on raw floats.
