Not yet done: real (non-synthetic) simulation data, 2D image/pressure-field support beyond the vector-curl case, a proper container/CLI, and a combined (not per-component) energy invariant across u/v/w — noted as a known simplification in the current constraint layer.
Open source plan
• License: Apache 2.0 or MIT — permissive, so it's easy to adopt inside lab and HPC-center pipelines that mix licenses.
• Repo structure: keep the four stages as separate modules (attention / bitpack / constraint / backend) with a thin pipeline orchestrator, matching the current prototype layout — makes it easy for contributors to swap in a new attention function or a different entropy backend without touching the rest.
• First releases: ship the prototype as-is with a clear "research code" label, a synthetic benchmark suite, and a couple of real-world example datasets (public CFD or cosmology snapshots) so people can validate against their own data before trusting it.
• Roadmap after that: combined multi-component energy invariant, a CLI and a simple file container format, pluggable attention functions (Q-criterion, curvature, domain-specific detectors), and a comparison harness against SZ/ZFP/TTHRESH on standard scientific-compression benchmark corpora (e.g. SDRBench).
• Call for contributors: most useful early help is domain scientists trying it on real simulation output and reporting where the attention map or the conserved-quantity choice doesn't match what their field actually needs preserved — the attention function and the constraint metric are both meant to be swapped per domain, not fixed.
